.TH tar_set_gnu_extension_limits 3 "Nov 2026" "University of Illinois" "C Library Calls"
.SH NAME
tar_set_gnu_extension_limits \- set bounds on GNU long-name extensions per record
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <libtar.h>
.P
.BI "int tar_set_gnu_extension_limits(TAR *" t ", size_t " bytes ", size_t " headers ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBtar_set_gnu_extension_limits\fP() function configures how much GNU
\fBL\fP/\fBK\fP extension data a single logical record may carry on the
handle \fIt\fP, which must have been created by \fBtar_open\fP() or
\fBtar_fdopen\fP().

\fIbytes\fP bounds the sum of the declared payload lengths of every
extension header read before one ordinary header, including extension
payloads later superseded by another extension of the same type.
\fIheaders\fP bounds the number of such extension headers.  Both
arguments must be greater than zero.

The defaults for every new handle are 1 MiB of declared payload and 64
extension headers.  The counters are recomputed for every logical record.
Limits and accumulated state belong to the handle, so two open handles do
not share budgets.

The function may only be called between two \fBth_read\fP() calls.  It
does not clear a read-error state left by a failed read.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The function returns 0 on success.  On failure it returns \-1 and sets
\fIerrno\fP.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
\fBEINVAL\fP
Either limit was zero, \fIt\fP was NULL, or the handle is in a read-error
state.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR th_read (3),
.BR tar_open (3)
